From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 14:35:31 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 09935/11362] Measure a recommended test, decline it, and
 record why

The team lead recommended a stronger tamper test: edit a RECORDED receipt value
inside an artifact-shaped payload rather than compare two unrelated hashes. Built
it and measured its unique kills before shipping it. There are none.

  constant hash                  candidate RED, alongside 10 existing tests
  revert to working-tree resolve candidate GREEN, 6 existing tests RED

So it duplicates coverage the suite already has and misses coverage the suite
already has. Shipping it would add a test no mutation kills uniquely, which is
the exact bug class the tamper fix at d23b873abd removed. Declined on that
evidence rather than on preference.

The case it was meant to document is not uncovered. It is proved where it can
bite: break case 4 against exp5355, editing the stored
source_artifact_checksums.spec, which takes the real replay test red and returns
green on restore. A helper-level version cannot be lethal, because the helper
never sees the recorded value -- the comparison lives in the caller.

Precedent, which is worth more than the fix: mutation testing caught four things
today that review did not, twice on the finder's own work. Here it also decided
against writing code. The standing rule -- prove each pattern is under test by
deleting it -- already covers this; today is evidence it earns its keep in both
directions.

